A||N||3||MickeyAsh||Starter motor doesnt always turn||||9||
Z||000000||MickeyAsh||12-10-2000||02:09 AM||||Ahh... the joys of automobiles... lately when I go to crank my little baby up, as I turn the key all the way forward, she doesnt wanna crank... and I dont mean dead battery type stuff, the starter doesnt even roll... the interior lights go dim, the idiot light comes on, and no starter, no "rrr rrrrr", nothing... sometimes it goes away immediately, sometimes I have to move the car around a bit... tonight I had to physically tinker with the engine to make progress, and in the process I noticed the the ground wires leading from my battery leads to the body of the car, were hot, I also smelled burning plastic... the smell and heat quickly went away... when I finally got it to crank, when I first turned the key, the starter was jumpy, like the engine was already cranked, or like it was being turned on and off rapidly... like it was arcing or something... past that, she rolled right over, fired up and purred like a kitty... my first thought would be a bad ground somewhere... or a wire jumping and grounding the starter... in which case, how do I go about testing wires, and where do I start? Any thoughts? Thanks in advance.<BR>-Sheep||||reg||9
Z||000001||PlasticWrapd||12-10-2000||02:44 AM||||Yep, had the same problem, but mine was two-fold.  I have an 84 w/2.5L and a 4-speed.  The first problem I found, which sounds like the problem you have, is the main ground wire (large black one that goes to the battery's negative terminal).  Mine runs from the battery to the cylinder head.  The end connected to the cylinder head was soaked with oil and gunk.  I just took it off and cleaned it.  First problem solved!  The second problem was the bolt that holds the starter on.  It's the longer of the two and screws into the block.  It was missing!  Yep, vibrated loose and snapped off.  I replaced it twice before I figured out what was happening.  The fix was to clean all the oil out of the bolt hole and off the bolt, coat the threads with anaerobic thread locking compound and <B>torque</B> the bolt to 30 Ft/Lbs.  Problem solved.  The bolt is still there and the ground problems are gone.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||12-10-2000||09:19 AM||||Sounds to me like it may be time for a new starter. That one may be drawing too much current, have it checked.||||reg||1
Z||000003||KRMFiero||12-10-2000||10:58 AM||||Hey if you dont hear a click then then solinoid might be gone||||reg||1
